    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-proper-continuous-map",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Proper continuous maps of topological spaces",
        "deps": [
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-continuous-map-top",
          "def-hausdorff-space",
          "def-locally-compact-space",
          "def-subspace-topology-top",
          "def-topological-space",
          "lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be topological spaces ([[def-topological-space]]) and let $f:X\\to Y$ be continuous ([[def-continuous-map-top]]). Then $f$ is **proper** when for every compact subset $K\\subseteq Y$ ([[def-compact-space]]) its preimage $f^{-1}(K)$ is a compact subset of $X$. Compactness of subsets is the intrinsic condition of [[def-compact-space]], namely compactness of the subspace $f^{-1}(K)$ with the subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]); by [[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]] it is equivalent to the ambient reading, so a subset is compact exactly when every cover by open subsets of the ambient space has a finite subcover, and the two readings are used interchangeably below without further comment. This is the notion of properness used throughout the analytic six-operation development on this page, where the spaces involved are locally compact Hausdorff ([[def-locally-compact-space]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]). The stability properties consumed by the later items — closed inclusions are proper, composites of proper maps are proper, properness is preserved by base change along a continuous map into a Hausdorff space, and fibres and closed subspaces of a proper map inherit properness — are proved in the lemma `lem-proper-continuous-map-stability` on this page. The definition involves no choice principle: it quantifies over compact subsets and preimages only.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jean-Louis Verdier, Dualité dans la cohomologie des espaces localement compacts, Séminaire N. Bourbaki 1964–1966, exposé 300, pp. 337–349 (Novembre 1965)",
              "url": "https://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1964-1966__9__337_0.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "The Stacks Project, Cohomology of Sheaves (chapter 20): §12 flasque sheaves, §16 cohomology on Hausdorff quasi-compact spaces, §17 the base change map, §18 proper base change in topology, §21 cohomology with support in a closed subset, §54 projection formula",
              "url": "https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/cohomology.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "No choice principle is used: the definition quantifies over compact subsets and preimages only.",
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-proper-continuous-map-stability",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Basic stability of proper continuous maps",
        "deps": [
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-continuous-map-top",
          "def-hausdorff-space",
          "def-initial-and-final-topology",
          "def-locally-compact-space",
          "def-product-topology",
          "def-proper-continuous-map",
          "def-subspace-topology-top",
          "def-the-diagonal-of-a-space",
          "def-topological-space",
          "lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient",
          "thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact",
          "thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed",
          "thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps",
          "thm-finite-products-of-compact-spaces",
          "thm-hausdorff-iff-the-diagonal-is-closed",
          "thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $X$, $Y$ and $Z$ be topological spaces ([[def-topological-space]]), with properness as in [[def-proper-continuous-map]]. 1. If $i:Z\\hookrightarrow X$ is the inclusion of a closed subset, then $i$ is proper. 2. If $f:X\\to Y$ and $g:Y\\to Z$ are proper continuous maps, then the composite $gf:X\\to Z$ is proper. 3. If $f:X\\to Y$ is proper, $Y$ is Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]) and $u:Y'\\to Y$ is continuous, then the pullback $$f':X\\times_YY'\\longrightarrow Y',\\qquad X\\times_YY'=\\{(x,y')\\in X\\times Y': f(x)=u(y')\\},$$ the subset carrying the subspace topology of the product ([[def-product-topology]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]), is proper. 4. If $f:X\\to Y$ is proper, then every fibre $f^{-1}(y)$ is a compact subset of $X$. 5. If $f:X\\to Y$ is proper and $S\\subseteq X$ is closed, then the restriction $f|_S:S\\to Y$ is proper. 6. If $f:X\\to Y$ is proper and $Y$ is locally compact Hausdorff ([[def-locally-compact-space]]), then $f$ is a closed map: the image of every closed subset of $X$ is closed in $Y$. These are the stability properties consumed by the closed-support functor $f_!$ on this page; no choice principle is used in any of them.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jean-Louis Verdier, Dualité dans la cohomologie des espaces localement compacts, Séminaire N. Bourbaki 1964–1966, exposé 300, pp. 337–349 (Novembre 1965)",
              "url": "https://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1964-1966__9__337_0.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "The Stacks Project, Cohomology of Sheaves (chapter 20): §12 flasque sheaves, §16 cohomology on Hausdorff quasi-compact spaces, §17 the base change map, §18 proper base change in topology, §21 cohomology with support in a closed subset, §54 projection formula",
              "url": "https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/cohomology.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "No choice principle is used; the compactness arguments are finite or reduce to closedness of compact subsets in Hausdorff spaces.",
        "dependency_level": 1
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-bounded-analytic-field-sheaves-have-derived-tensor-hom-and-pullback-pushforward-adjunctions",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Derived tensor-Hom and inverse/direct image for field sheaves",
        "deps": [
          "cor-the-cone-criterion-from-the-general-long-exact-sequence",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-bounded-bounded-below-and-bounded-above-complex",
          "def-derived-category-of-an-abelian-category",
          "def-derived-hom-in-the-bounded-setting",
          "def-direct-image-sheaf",
          "def-extension-by-zero-abelian-sheaf",
          "def-flasque-sheaf",
          "def-inverse-image-presheaf-and-sheaf",
          "def-sheaf-cohomology-derived-global-sections",
          "def-sheaf-hom",
          "def-sheaf-on-topological-space",
          "def-sheaf-tensor-product",
          "lem-acyclic-assembly-by-exact-columns",
          "lem-bounded-below-complexes-admit-injective-replacements",
          "lem-flasque-kernel-lifts-quotient-sections",
          "lem-ringed-space-module-sheaves-enough-injectives",
          "lem-stalk-inverse-image-sheaf",
          "prop-positive-right-derived-functors-vanish-on-injective-objects",
          "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice",
          "thm-exactness-of-sheaves-stalkwise",
          "thm-extension-by-zero-adjunction-exactness",
          "thm-injective-complexes-model-the-bounded-below-derived-category",
          "thm-inverse-direct-image-adjunction",
          "thm-long-exact-sequence-in-cohomology",
          "thm-right-derived-functors-form-a-cohomological-delta-functor",
          "thm-sheafification-preserves-stalks"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $f:X\\to Y$ be a continuous map of topological spaces and let $k$ be a field. Inverse image of sheaves of $k$-vector spaces ([[def-sheaf-on-topological-space]], [[def-inverse-image-presheaf-and-sheaf]]) is exact and $f^*\\dashv Rf_*$, where $Rf_*$ is computed on bounded-below $k$-linear injective replacements ([[lem-bounded-below-complexes-admit-injective-replacements]], [[thm-injective-complexes-model-the-bounded-below-derived-category]]). For bounded complexes $A,B$ ([[def-bounded-bounded-below-and-bounded-above-complex]]) the derived tensor over $k$ is their signed total tensor ([[def-sheaf-tensor-product]]), and for bounded $A$ and bounded-below $B$ the derived internal Hom $R\\mathcal Hom(A,B)$ is computed by $\\mathcal Hom^\\bullet(A,I_B)$ in a $k$-linear bounded-below injective model $B\\to I_B$ ([[def-sheaf-hom]], [[def-derived-hom-in-the-bounded-setting]]). Flasque $k$-sheaves ([[def-flasque-sheaf]]) are acyclic for sections on every open set ([[def-sheaf-cohomology-derived-global-sections]]). The tensor-Hom adjunction and the dg associativity, evaluation and symmetry identities are natural. No bounded-constructibility claim for the internal Hom is made here; that requires the local geometric supplier of the later constructible items.",
        "strategy": "Assume AC. Route: read the ordinary operations off stalks, construct the signed total tensor and the injective Hom model, prove flasque acyclicity from the flasque-kernel lifting theorem, and finish with the chain-Hom adjunction. Sources: Verdier, expose 300, SS1 pp. 339-341 (injective models in $D^+$ and the basic properties of $f_!$) and Stacks Project, Cohomology of Sheaves, SS12 (Definition 12.1, Lemma 12.2, Lemma 12.3, Lemma 12.5). Step 1 (inverse and direct image). The stalk of $f^{-1}G$ at $x$ is $G_{f(x)}$ ([[lem-stalk-inverse-image-sheaf]]), so $f^*=f^{-1}$ is exact and preserves quasi-isomorphisms; the inverse/direct-image adjunction ([[thm-inverse-direct-image-adjunction]]) is $k$-linear, and for injective $I$ on $X$ the functor $\\operatorname{Hom}_Y(-,f_*I)$ is naturally $\\operatorname{Hom}_X(f^*(-),I)$, so $f_*I$ is injective and the chain-Hom adjunction gives $f^*\\dashv Rf_*$ on $D^+$. Step 2 (tensor). Define $F\\otimes_kG$ by sheafifying the sectionwise tensor presheaf; its stalks are $F_x\\otimes_kG_x$ ([[def-sheaf-tensor-product]]) because filtered colimits commute with finite tensor expressions. All $k$-vector spaces are flat, so tensoring is stalk-exact, and a bounded complex is K-flat: filter the finite-column total tensor against an acyclic complex by $A$-degree and use that each column is acyclic. Hence the signed total tensor of bounded representatives computes $A\\otimes^L_kB$ independently of the representatives. Step 3 (internal Hom). For a bounded-below injective replacement $B\\to I_B$ and a bounded representative $A$, put $R\\mathcal Hom(A,B)=\\mathcal Hom^\\bullet(A,I_B)$ with $d(\\varphi)=d_I\\varphi-(-1)^n\\varphi d_A$; each $\\mathcal Hom(A^p,I^q)$ is injective because it is right adjoint to the exact functor $-\\otimes A^p$, the total complex is bounded below and termwise injective, and replacing $A$ or $I_B$ changes it only by a quasi-isomorphism (test on the open extensions $j_U!$ and use stalk exactness). This is the actual internal derived Hom, not a sectionwise Hom of stalks. Step 4 (flasque acyclicity). An injective $k$-sheaf is flasque since $\\operatorname{Hom}(j_U!k_U,I)=I(U)$ and injectivity extends sections along $j_U!k_U\\to j_V!k_V$ for $U\\subset V$. Embed a flasque $F$ into an injective $I$; the flasque-kernel lifting theorem ([[lem-flasque-kernel-lifts-quotient-sections]]) makes the quotient flasque with $I(U)\\to Q(U)$ onto for every open $U$, so the long exact sequence gives $H^1(U,F)=0$ and $H^{n+1}(U,F)\\cong H^n(U,Q)$; induction on degree proves vanishing, and for a bounded-below flasque complex the same degreewise comparison computes derived sections on each open by the ordinary section complex. Step 5 (adjunction identities). The signed chain transposition $\\mathcal Hom^\\bullet(C\\otimes A,I)\\cong\\mathcal Hom^\\bullet(C,\\mathcal Hom^\\bullet(A,I))$ is an isomorphism (Koszul reordering), and since $I$ and $\\mathcal Hom^\\bullet(A,I)$ are K-injective and bounded $C$ is K-flat, $H^0$ yields tensor $\\dashv$ $R\\mathcal Hom$ on $D^b$ with bounded-below target; restriction to opens gives the internal version, and associativity, symmetry, evaluation and currying hold on pure tensors and hence on sheaves. No constructibility of the internal Hom is asserted; that is supplied later on this page by the geometric finite conical/cochain models and the derived incidence model, which are not prerequisites of this lemma.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jean-Louis Verdier, Dualite dans la cohomologie des espaces localement compacts, Seminaire N. Bourbaki 1964-1966, expose 300, pp. 337-349 (Novembre 1965)",
              "url": "https://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1964-1966__9__337_0.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "The Stacks Project, Cohomology of Sheaves (chapter 20): SS12 Flasque sheaves, SS16 Cohomology on Hausdorff quasi-compact spaces, SS17 The base change map, SS18 Proper base change in topology, SS21 Cohomology with support in a closed subset, SS54 Projection formula",
              "url": "https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/cohomology.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "AC is assumed and used exactly as follows: [[thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice]] derives DC from AC, which feeds the bounded-below injective replacement [[lem-bounded-below-complexes-admit-injective-replacements]] and the injective model [[thm-injective-complexes-model-the-bounded-below-derived-category]]; [[lem-ringed-space-module-sheaves-enough-injectives]] consumes AC through Grothendieck's injective-embedding theorem; [[lem-flasque-kernel-lifts-quotient-sections]] consumes AC through Zorn's lemma. The stalk, tensor, Hom and adjunction verifications are choice-free.",
        "dependency_level": 8
      },
      {
        "id": "def-derived-operations-on-analytic-sheaves-over-locally-compact-spaces",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Derived operations on analytic field sheaves",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-c-soft-sheaf",
          "def-compactly-supported-global-sections",
          "def-derived-category-of-an-abelian-category",
          "def-direct-image-sheaf",
          "def-extension-by-zero-abelian-sheaf",
          "def-flasque-sheaf",
          "def-hausdorff-space",
          "def-inverse-image-presheaf-and-sheaf",
          "def-locally-compact-space",
          "def-proper-continuous-map",
          "def-sheaf-cohomology-derived-global-sections",
          "def-sheaf-hom",
          "def-sheaf-on-topological-space",
          "def-sheaf-tensor-product",
          "def-support-of-a-sheaf-section",
          "lem-bounded-analytic-field-sheaves-have-derived-tensor-hom-and-pullback-pushforward-adjunctions",
          "lem-c-soft-field-sheaves-are-compact-support-acyclic",
          "lem-finite-fibre-dimension-gives-bounded-fibrewise-soft-resolutions",
          "lem-proper-continuous-map-stability"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $k$ be a field and let $X,Y$ be locally compact Hausdorff spaces ([[def-locally-compact-space]], [[def-hausdorff-space]]). Write $\\operatorname{Sh}_k(X)$ for the category of sheaves of $k$-vector spaces ([[def-sheaf-on-topological-space]], [[def-sheaf-cohomology-derived-global-sections]]) viewed as modules over the constant ring sheaf $k_X$, and form derived categories in those sheaves ([[def-derived-category-of-an-abelian-category]]). For a continuous map $f:X\\to Y$, set $f^*=f^{-1}$ ([[def-inverse-image-presheaf-and-sheaf]]) and define $f_!F$ on an open $V\\subseteq Y$ to consist of the sections of $F$ on $f^{-1}(V)$ whose closed support ([[def-support-of-a-sheaf-section]], [[def-compactly-supported-global-sections]]) maps properly to $V$, properness being that of [[def-proper-continuous-map]] together with its stability lemma [[lem-proper-continuous-map-stability]]. Under AC ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), $Rf_*$ and $Rf_!$ are computed on bounded-below $k$-linear injective replacements. Tensor means the signed total tensor over $k$ ([[def-sheaf-tensor-product]]) and internal derived Hom is the injective construction of [[lem-bounded-analytic-field-sheaves-have-derived-tensor-hom-and-pullback-pushforward-adjunctions]] ([[def-sheaf-hom]]). Suppose the uniform all-fibre-open cohomological dimension bound of [[lem-finite-fibre-dimension-gives-bounded-fibrewise-soft-resolutions]] holds, and choose its finite resolution $k_X\\to K$; for an open $U\\subseteq X$ put $K_U=j_{U!}j_U^*K$ ([[def-extension-by-zero-abelian-sheaf]], [[def-c-soft-sheaf]]). For a bounded-below injective complex $I$ on $Y$, define the exceptional inverse-image candidate to be the sheaf complex with open sections $\\operatorname{Hom}_Y^\\bullet(f_!K_U,I)$. [[thm-compactly-supported-derived-pushforward-has-exceptional-inverse-image-adjoint]] proves that this candidate is a sheaf complex and is independent of the chosen resolution. Set $\\omega_X=a_X^!k$ and $D_XA=R\\mathcal Hom(A,\\omega_X)$ when the terminal map satisfies the same finite-dimensional hypotheses. Constructible restriction is a separate geometric theorem; none of these definitions asserts a generic six-functor axiom. The prescriptions are those of Verdier's exposé: $f^*$ is the exact inverse image, $f_!$ is the sheaf of sections whose closed support is proper over the base — a presheaf by the local nature of properness, whose sheaf property on bounded-below injective inputs is proved by the exceptional-adjoint theorem recorded in `justified_by` — $Rf_*$ is derived from the direct image [[def-direct-image-sheaf]] on bounded-below injective replacements, and the terms of the chosen finite resolution are $c$-soft and hence acyclic for compact support ([[lem-c-soft-field-sheaves-are-compact-support-acyclic]]). The ordinary-operations lemma also supplies the flasque acyclicity ([[def-flasque-sheaf]]) used to compare resolutions. AC is used only for injective replacements; the proper-support sections, extension by zero and the exceptional candidate itself are choice-free constructions. Cohomological shifts obey $H^i(A[r])=H^{i+r}(A)$ in the convention of [[def-derived-category-of-an-abelian-category]].",
        "strategy": "This item is definitional: it fixes the categorical setting, the functors, and the exceptional candidate, and its only well-definedness obligation is the exceptional-inverse-image adjoint theorem of the next item on this page (recorded as that definition's justified_by target at authoring, not as a prerequisite of this definition). Source route: Verdier, expose 300, SS1-SS2 pp. 339-343 (definition and left exactness of $f_!$, the finite fibrewise-$c$-soft resolution $k_X\\to K$, and the construction of $f^!$ from $\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_Y(f_!K_U,I)$), and Stacks Project, Cohomology of Sheaves SS12 (flasque acyclicity, used by the resolution). Step 1 fixes $\\operatorname{Sh}_k(X)$ as an abelian category of module sheaves over $k_X$ and its derived category. Step 2 defines $f^*=f^{-1}$, $f_!$ by proper support of sections, $Rf_*$ and $Rf_!$ by bounded-below injective replacements, and recalls $\\otimes_k^L$ and $R\\mathcal Hom$ from the ordinary-operations lemma. Step 3 assumes the uniform all-fibre-open bound, fixes the finite resolution $k_X\\to K$ of the bounded-fibrewise-soft-resolution lemma, and forms $K_U=j_{U!}j_U^*K$ for opens $U$. Step 4 defines the exceptional inverse-image candidate by the section complexes $\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_Y(f_!K_U,I)$; the adjoint theorem proves this is a sheaf and independent of the choices, so the definition is well posed. Step 5 defines $\\omega_X=a_X^!k$ and $D_X=R\\mathcal Hom(-,\\omega_X)$ under the same finite-dimensional hypotheses. No generic six-functor axiom, no constructible restriction and no unrestricted right adjunction is asserted here.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jean-Louis Verdier, Dualite dans la cohomologie des espaces localement compacts, Seminaire N. Bourbaki 1964-1966, expose 300, pp. 337-349 (Novembre 1965)",
              "url": "https://www.numdam.org/item/SB_1964-1966__9__337_0.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "The Stacks Project, Cohomology of Sheaves (chapter 20): SS12 Flasque sheaves, SS16 Cohomology on Hausdorff quasi-compact spaces, SS17 The base change map, SS18 Proper base change in topology, SS21 Cohomology with support in a closed subset, SS54 Projection formula",
              "url": "https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/download/cohomology.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "axiom_use": "AC is declared because the definition of $Rf_*$ and $Rf_!$ through bounded-below injective replacements consumes the enough-injectives embedding for module sheaves over the constant ring sheaf (inside [[lem-bounded-analytic-field-sheaves-have-derived-tensor-hom-and-pullback-pushforward-adjunctions]]). The proper-support sections, extension by zero and the exceptional candidate itself are choice-free constructions.",
        "dependency_level": 13,
        "justified_by": [
          "thm-compactly-supported-derived-pushforward-has-exceptional-inverse-image-adjoint"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-compactly-supported-derived-pushforward-has-exceptional-inverse-image-adjoint",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Exceptional inverse image as the adjoint of derived proper-support image",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-c-soft-sheaf",
          "def-compactly-supported-global-sections",
          "def-derived-category-of-an-abelian-category",
          "def-derived-hom-in-the-bounded-setting",
          "def-derived-operations-on-analytic-sheaves-over-locally-compact-spaces",
          "def-direct-image-sheaf",
          "def-extension-by-zero-abelian-sheaf",
          "def-flasque-sheaf",
          "def-inverse-image-presheaf-and-sheaf",
          "def-proper-continuous-map",
          "def-sheaf-hom",
          "def-sheaf-on-topological-space",
          "def-sheaf-tensor-product",
          "def-support-of-a-sheaf-section",
          "lem-abelian-sheaves-admit-bounded-above-flat-resolutions",
          "lem-bounded-analytic-field-sheaves-have-derived-tensor-hom-and-pullback-pushforward-adjunctions",
          "lem-bounded-below-complexes-admit-injective-replacements",
          "lem-c-soft-field-sheaves-are-compact-support-acyclic",
          "lem-finite-fibre-dimension-gives-bounded-fibrewise-soft-resolutions",
          "lem-proper-continuous-map-stability",
          "lem-sections-near-a-compact-set-are-sections-on-that-set",
          "thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact",
          "thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed",
          "thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps",
          "thm-extension-by-zero-adjunction-exactness",
          "thm-flasque-sheaves-acyclic",
          "thm-locally-compact-hausdorff-basics"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume AC ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) and the uniform all-fibre-open dimension bound $d$ of [[lem-finite-fibre-dimension-gives-bounded-fibrewise-soft-resolutions]]. For a continuous map $f:X\\to Y$ of locally compact Hausdorff spaces, the proper-support functor $f_!$ of [[def-derived-operations-on-analytic-sheaves-over-locally-compact-spaces]] is left exact, satisfies $(Rf_!A)_y\\simeq R\\Gamma_c(X_y,A|_{X_y})$ on every fibre, and maps bounded derived categories into bounded derived categories. Its exceptional inverse-image candidate $f^!$ represents the natural Hom transposition $$\\operatorname{Hom}(Rf_!A,B)\\simeq\\operatorname{Hom}(A,f^!B)$$ for bounded source objects $A$ and bounded-below target objects $B$, and has lower cohomological bound $\\inf(B)-d$. When the later geometric theorem proves that $f^!$ preserves the bounded constructible subcategory, this becomes the right adjunction $Rf_!\\dashv f^!$ there; on that bounded constructible specialization the adjunction unit, counit and resolution comparisons satisfy the triangle, identity and cocycle laws. The proper-support evaluation constructs the counit directly. The proof constructs this adjoint from a finite fibrewise-soft resolution and generator complexes, independently of Verdier biduality; it does not infer an unrestricted $D^+$-source adjunction from the bounded-source generator argument.",
        "strategy": "Assume AC. Sources: Verdier, expose 300, SS1 pp. 339-341 (left exactness, proper-support stalk formula, finite cohomological dimension, $Y$-mou resolutions) and SS2 pp. 341-343 (the resolution $k_X\\to K$, the complex $\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_Y(f_!K_U,I)$, flasqueness and the evaluation $\\gamma$); Stacks Project, Cohomology of Sheaves SS12 (flasque acyclicity), SS16-SS18 (compact-neighbourhood continuity and ordinary proper base change as an independent check), SS21 (localization). Step 1: $f_!$ is a sheaf, left exact, equals $f_*$ for proper $f$ and extension by zero / direct image for open / closed inclusions. Step 2: the stalk map $(f_!A)_y\\to\\Gamma_c(X_y,A|_{X_y})$ is injective and surjective by the compact-neighbourhood gluing lemma [[lem-sections-near-a-compact-set-are-sections-on-that-set]] and the collar argument. Step 3: for injective $A$ the fibre restrictions are $c$-soft ([[lem-c-soft-field-sheaves-are-compact-support-acyclic]]), so $(Rf_!A)_y\\simeq R\\Gamma_c(X_y,A|_{X_y})$ and the finite bound gives $Rf_!:D^b\\to D^b$ with fibrewise-$c$-soft acyclicity. Step 4: choose the finite resolution $k_X\\to K$ in degrees $0,\\dots,d$, set $K_U=j_{U!}j_U^*K$, and form $H_K(I)(U)=\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_Y(f_!K_U,I)$; the augmented extension-by-zero Cech complex is exact on stalks and its cycles are $f_!$-acyclic by dimension shifting past the bound $d$, so $H_K(I)$ is a flasque sheaf complex with lower bound $\\inf(I)-d$. Step 5: for $E_U=j_{U!}k_U$ the complex $K_U$ resolves $E_U$ by $f_!$-acyclic terms, giving $R\\operatorname{Hom}_X(E_U,H_K(I))=\\operatorname{Hom}^\\bullet_Y(f_!K_U,I)$; the descending kernel recursion from [[lem-abelian-sheaves-admit-bounded-above-flat-resolutions]] builds a bounded-above generator complex $M\\to A$ quasi-isomorphic and stalk-surjective over $k$, and the bounded-diagonal Hom comparison against an injective replacement of $H_K(I)$ proves $R\\operatorname{Hom}_X(A,H_K(I))\\simeq R\\operatorname{Hom}_Y(Rf_!A,I)$ for bounded $A$ and bounded-below $I$. Step 6: $H^0$ gives the Hom transposition, $f^!B:=H_K(I_B)$ is the represented adjoint, and the counit is constructed directly from $k_V\\to f_*k_Q\\to f_*(K|Q)$ over closed $Q$ proper over $V$; the flasque terms are $f_!$-acyclic, and on the bounded constructible specialization (after the geometric bounded-output theorem) the triangle and cocycle laws follow by inverse transposition. The bounded-source generator argument alone is not called an unrestricted adjunction.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
