          "def-sheaf-on-topological-space",
          "def-sheaf-tensor-product",
          "def-support-of-a-sheaf-section",
          "lem-bounded-analytic-field-sheaves-have-derived-tensor-hom-and-pullback-pushforward-adjunctions",
          "lem-c-soft-field-sheaves-are-compact-support-acyclic",
          "lem-extension-by-zero-short-exact-sequence",
          "lem-proper-continuous-map-stability",
          "lem-sections-near-a-compact-set-are-sections-on-that-set",
          "lem-sheaf-supported-on-a-closed-subset-is-a-pushforward",
          "thm-compactly-supported-derived-pushforward-has-exceptional-inverse-image-adjoint",
          "thm-extension-by-zero-adjunction-exactness",
          "thm-inverse-direct-image-adjunction"
        ],
        "statement": "Under the preceding locally compact and finite-dimensional hypotheses, $u^*Rf_!\\simeq Rf'_!v^*$ for Cartesian squares; proper maps have $Rf_!=Rf_*$, giving proper ordinary base change. Bounded tensor factors satisfy the projection formula. Proper-support direct images compose canonically. On the bounded constructible specialization proved by the subsequent local duality construction, exceptional adjoints also compose canonically; their units, counits and traces respect composition and Cartesian pasting. For a closed inclusion $i:Z\\to X$ with complement $j$, $i^!$ is the derived functor of $i^{-1}\\Gamma_Z$, and both localization triangles hold. Locally, $R\\mathcal Hom(i_*k_Z,A)$ is $R\\Gamma_ZA$. This lemma does not assume or claim extraordinary dual base change before constructible biduality.",
        "strategy": "Assume AC. Sources: Verdier, expose 300, SS1.2 p. 340 (base change), SS1.5 p. 341 (compose), SS2.3 p. 342-343 (the trace $\\gamma:\\ f_!f^!(G)\\to G$) and SS2 p. 341-342 (flasque terms); Stacks Project, Cohomology of Sheaves SS17-SS18 (base change map and proper base change, as an independent verification of the canonical exchange) and SS21 (the localization triangle of $R\\Gamma_Z$). Step 1 (closed support). Put $\\Gamma_ZF=\\ker(F\\to j_*j^*F)$; it has zero stalks off $Z$, hence equals $i_*i^{-1}\\Gamma_ZF$ by [[lem-sheaf-supported-on-a-closed-subset-is-a-pushforward]], and $i^!_0=i^{-1}\\Gamma_Z$ is right adjoint to the exact $i_*$ and preserves injectives; the stalk exact sequence from [[lem-extension-by-zero-short-exact-sequence]] gives both localization triangles, and tensor-Hom transposition with the closed adjunction identifies $R\\mathcal Hom(i_*k_Z,A)$ with $R\\Gamma_ZA$ locally on every open. Step 2 (base change). For a Cartesian square, inverse image of an injective on $X$ restricts on each fibre of $f'$ to the restriction of the original injective, hence is fibrewise-$c$-soft and $f'_!$-acyclic; the fibre-stalk formula of the exceptional-adjunction theorem identifies $u^*f_!I\\to f'_!v^*I$ on stalks, and applying to an injective complex proves $u^*Rf_!\\simeq Rf'_!v^*$; for proper $f$ this is ordinary proper base change. Step 3 (projection). On fibres, $f^*B$ is the constant complex $B_y$ and tensoring a $c$-soft complex with a vector space stays $c$-soft, with $\\Gamma_c(E\\otimes V)=\\Gamma_c(E)\\otimes V$ for finite tensor presentations; this proves the canonical map $Rf_!A\\otimes B\\to Rf_!(A\\otimes f^*B)$ is a stalk isomorphism and identifies it with the adjoint of the counit tensored with $\\mathrm{id}$. Step 4 (composition and traces). The support definition proves $(gf)_!=g_!f_!$ (proper support over $Y$ and $Z$ agree by the two properness conditions), and applying the two support functors to an injective resolution gives the canonical composition isomorphism; the trace homomorphisms are the actual counits of the exceptional adjunction, and $\\mathrm{tr}_{gf}=\\mathrm{tr}_g\\circ Rg_!(\\mathrm{tr}_f)g^!$ follows by transposing the identity of $f^!g^!$. Step 5 (pasting). The two composites of base-change maps in a pasted square restrict a representative section to the same fibre section before deriving, and both projection composites send a pure tensor to the same pullback tensor; all identities hold in the dg resolution model. Extraordinary proper exchange $u^!Rf_*\\simeq Rf'_*v^!$ is deliberately NOT claimed here: it is proved only after constructible biduality on this page, in the later duality theorem that is not a prerequisite of this lemma.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Jean-Louis Verdier, Dualite dans la cohomologie des espaces localement compacts, Seminaire N. Bourbaki 1964-1966, expose 300, pp. 337-349 (Novembre 1965)",
