      {
        "id": "thm-ordinary-middle-perverse-t-structure-glues-and-has-simple-intersection-complexes",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Ordinary middle perverse t structure, recollement and intersection complexes",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-canonical-t-structure-on-a-derived-category",
          "def-derived-category-of-an-abelian-category",
          "def-derived-hom-in-the-bounded-setting",
          "def-direct-image-sheaf",
          "def-extension-by-zero-abelian-sheaf",
          "def-inverse-image-presheaf-and-sheaf",
          "def-locally-constant-sheaf-of-finite-rank",
          "def-sheaf-on-topological-space",
          "lem-analytic-constructible-sheaves-admit-coherent-six-operations",
          "lem-complex-algebraic-stratifications-have-finite-conical-cochain-models",
          "lem-cone-triangles-satisfy-the-octahedral-axiom",
          "prop-octahedral-gives-a-triangle-relating-the-cones-of-f-g-and-gf"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume AC ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) and let $k$ be any field. On $D_c^b(X;k)$ for a separated finite-type complex variety $X$, middle perversity is given on smooth complex strata $S$ by the two inequalities $H^q(i_S^*A)=0$ for $q>-\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S$ and $H^q(i_S^!A)=0$ for $q<-\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S$. These inequalities define a bounded t structure ([[def-canonical-t-structure-on-a-derived-category]]) on $D_c^b(X;k)$, independent of compatible refinement and gluing across algebraic open/closed decompositions. Verdier duality exchanges its two aisles. The heart is abelian and of finite length, and its simple objects are the intersection complexes $j_{!*}L[\\dim_{\\mathbb C}Z]$ of simple finite-rank local systems $L$ ([[def-locally-constant-sheaf-of-finite-rank]]) on dense smooth opens of irreducible supports $Z$. Open restriction is perverse exact. Heart objects have no negative Hom, and the endomorphism ring of a simple object is a division $k$-algebra. This is the ORDINARY category: no equivariant category, no normalization, no semisimplicity and no noncharacteristic theorem is used or asserted.",
        "strategy": "Assume AC. Sources: BBD, Faisceaux pervers, SS1.4.1-1.4.26 (recollement: Theoreme 1.4.10 constructing the glued t-structure from t-structures on an open $U$ and its closed complement, and the definitions/properties of $j_!$, $j_*$, $i^*$, $i^!$) and SS2.1.1-2.1.23 (perversity on a stratified space, the perverse t-structure of Proposition 2.1.3, the intermediate extension $j_{!*}$ of Definition 1.4.22/Corollaries 1.4.24-1.4.25, and the simple objects of Proposition 1.4.26); Bernstein-Lunts, Part I SS1.4.5 (open localization triangles) as a second treatment of the recollement input. Step 1: the six-operation supplier gives $j_!\\dashv j^*\\dashv Rj_*$ and $i^*\\dashv i_*\\dashv i^!$, full faithfulness of $i_*$ and $j_!$, $j^*i_*=0$ and the two localization triangles; defining $T^{\\le0}$ by $j^*K\\le0$, $i^*K\\le0$ and $T^{\\ge0}$ by $j^*K\\ge0$, $i^!K\\ge0$, orthogonality follows from $j_!j^*A\\to A\\to i_*i^*A$ and truncation is built through the standard octahedral completion; induction on the finite number of strata, starting from the ordinary t-structure shifted by $\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S$ on each smooth stratum, proves the bounded t-structure stays in $D_c^b$. Step 2: for a refinement $T\\subset S$ of complex codimension $c$, ordinary pullback preserves the upper bound $-\\dim_{\\mathbb C}S\\le-\\dim_{\\mathbb C}T$ and extraordinary pullback is ordinary pullback shifted by $-2c$, so the lower bound improves; both old halves lie in the refined halves and uniqueness of truncation gives equality on the old category, whence common refinement gives independence. Step 3: duality exchange $i_S^!D_XA=D_Si_S^*A$, $i_S^*D_XA=D_Si_S^!A$ together with the finite-dimensional dual $H^j(D_SK)=(H^{-j-2e}K)^\\vee$ for finite-rank local systems converts the upper inequality into the lower one and conversely, over every field, since $2e$ is an integral shift; applying the same argument to $D_XA$ and Verdier biduality gives equality of the exchanged aisles. Step 4: the heart is abelian because kernel and cokernel of a heart map are the perverse cohomologies in degrees $-1$ and $0$ of its cone; define $j_{!*}L$ as the image of ${}^pH^0j_!L\\to{}^pH^0Rj_*L$; its supported subobjects and quotients are removed by the intermediate-image construction using the localization triangles, and conversely any extension without boundary subobject or quotient is this image by adjunction. A subobject of $j_{!*}L$ restricts to a subobject of $L$, so for simple $L$ the intersection complex is simple; conversely a simple heart object has a nonzero restriction to a suitable open stratum which must be simple, and the no-boundary characterization identifies the object with its intermediate extension. On a fixed finite compatible stratification, finite-dimensional local systems have finite length, and induction through the two localization sequences proves finite length of the heart. Step 5: for $m<0$, $B[m]$ lies in $T^{\\ge1}$ and $A$ in $T^{\\le0}$, so orthogonality gives $\\operatorname{Hom}(A,B[m])=0$; a nonzero endomorphism of a simple object has kernel $0$ and image the whole object, hence is invertible, so its endomorphism ring is a division algebra over every field. No equivariant, semisimple or noncharacteristic input is used.",
        "provenance": {
