          "def-direct-image-sheaf",
          "def-extension-by-zero-abelian-sheaf",
          "def-inverse-image-presheaf-and-sheaf",
          "def-sheaf-on-topological-space",
          "lem-analytic-six-operation-support-and-trace-calculus",
          "thm-compactly-supported-derived-pushforward-has-exceptional-inverse-image-adjoint",
          "thm-verdier-duality-on-oriented-stratified-complex-varieties"
        ],
        "statement": "With the conventions of [[thm-verdier-duality-on-oriented-stratified-complex-varieties]] and coefficient field $k$: on the complex line $\\mathbb C$ (an oriented smooth complex 1-fold) the dualizing object is $\\omega_{\\mathbb C}=k_{\\mathbb C}[2]$. For the closed inclusion $i:\\{0\\}\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb C$ one has $i^!k_{\\mathbb C}=k[-2]$ and $i^!\\omega_{\\mathbb C}=k$. The example consumes the AC-assumed Verdier supplier; the displayed computation itself is choice-free.",
        "strategy": "Assume the conventions of the Verdier supplier (including AC). Sources: Verdier, expose 300, SS4-SS5 pp. 345-348 (local orientation and the $\\omega$ normalization for a real $n$-manifold), SS1.1 p. 339-340 (for a map to a point, $f_!$ is compactly supported sections), and Stacks Project, Cohomology of Sheaves SS21 (the localization triangle $R\\Gamma_Z\\to R\\Gamma\\to R\\Gamma_U$ identifying closed-support cohomology with relative cohomology of the pair). Step 1: a positively complex-oriented disk $D\\subset\\mathbb C$ has compact-support cochain complex with a single cohomology generator in degree $2$ over $k$, so the dualizing complex of the smooth complex $1$-fold is $k_{\\mathbb C}[2]$ by the orientation normalization $\\omega_X\\simeq k_X[2d]$ at $d=1$. Step 2: by [[lem-analytic-six-operation-support-and-trace-calculus]] the closed-support object is $i^!k_{\\mathbb C}=R\\Gamma_{\\{0\\}}(\\mathbb C,k_{\\mathbb C})$ (the derived functor of $i^{-1}\\Gamma_Z$ for the closed inclusion $i$), and by the Stacks localization triangle this is the relative cohomology of $(D,D\\setminus\\{0\\})$ for a small disk $D$; radial retraction of the punctured disk $D\\setminus\\{0\\}\\simeq S^1$ gives the relative cellular model with its single generator in degree $2$, so $i^!k_{\\mathbb C}=k[-2]$. Step 3: the same object is obtained by the duality route $i^!\\simeq D_{\\{0\\}}i^*D_{\\mathbb C}$ of [[thm-verdier-duality-on-oriented-stratified-complex-varieties]]: $D_{\\mathbb C}k_{\\mathbb C}=k_{\\mathbb C}[2]$, $i^*(k_{\\mathbb C}[2])=k[2]$ and $D_{\\{0\\}}(k[2])=k[-2]$. Applying the shift to $\\omega_{\\mathbb C}=k_{\\mathbb C}[2]$ gives $i^!\\omega_{\\mathbb C}=k[-2]\\,[2]=k$, consistently with dualizing transitivity and with the same positive orientation generator used in both computations.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
